{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773273704,"nonce":"8KXxyDg2k0A60vex9ytBZxKCQR4=","title":"Review container name collision with coder container","message":"When the watcher auto-resumes an orphaned review session, it launches a container with the coder's name (`nightshift-\u003csid\u003e`) instead of the review name (`nightshift-review-\u003csid\u003e`).\n\nThis causes:\n```\ndocker: Error response from daemon: Conflict. The container name \"/nightshift-c4c24c77afd6\" is already in use\n```\n\nThe review container and coder container must have distinct names. The review session ID is `review-\u003csid\u003e`, so `host/launch.py` should produce `nightshift-review-\u003csid\u003e` as the container name when launching a review session.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n1. Coder session hits max-turns and auto-resumes (container still running)\n2. Watcher detects orphaned review session and tries to auto-resume it\n3. Launch uses the same container name as the running coder container\n4. Docker rejects with name conflict\n\n## Expected\nReview containers named `nightshift-review-\u003csid\u003e`, coder containers named `nightshift-\u003csid\u003e`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Review and coder containers always have distinct names\n- [ ] No container name collisions during concurrent coder + reviewer runs","files":null}]}